You can remove the `(age X)` part (where X is any number) using a regular expression:

```js
const str = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";

// Remove "(age 7)" where 7 can be any number (or multiple digits)
const cleaned = str.replace(/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/i, '');

console.log(cleaned);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

Explanation of the regex `/\(age\s*\d+\)\s*/i`:

- `\(` and `\)` match the literal parentheses.
- `age` matches the word "age".
- `\s*` allows any spaces after `age`.
- `\d+` matches one or more digits (the age).
- The final `\s*` removes any trailing spaces after the `(age X)` part.
- `i` makes it case-insensitive (matches `age`, `Age`, etc.).

